{
  "comment": "Single source of truth for the optional ErrorEvent.retry_condition carrier (aci_protocol.events.RetryCondition) across the Python decoder, the generated Rust crate and the generated TypeScript types. Frames omit version: each reader stamps its own PROTOCOL_VERSION, so the version gate stays the only version check. Every accept frame must decode, and expected_retry_condition is the decoded carrier in canonical spelling (null, or the full reason, retry_at and scope_key object with null for an absent member), so the Python normalized dump and the Rust verbatim string agree. strict_construction refuse marks the one accept frame that tolerant decoders read but strict producer construction refuses (an unknown nested key). Every refuse frame must fail decode on its retry_condition alone; violation type is a shape TypeScript types also refuse, violation grammar is a well-typed string only the Python and Rust guards refuse. The scope_key digests are the sha256 of the example texts 'example credential scope' and 'example credential scope two', never a credential, account name or provider message. Read by packages/aci-protocol/tests/test_retry_condition.py (through aci_protocol.ts_vector_export.load_retry_condition_vector), by the retry_condition_tests module of packages/aci-protocol/generated/rust/src/lib.rs (rendered by packages/aci-protocol/src/aci_protocol/rust_export.py) and by packages/aci-protocol/src/aci_protocol/ts_vector_export.py, which renders packages/aci-protocol/generated/ts/retry-condition-vectors.ts for tsc. Every loader rejects unknown top-level and per-case keys, so a key one lane cannot see cannot pass vacuously. Changing a case here changes all three lanes.",
